[Music]  two years ago musician mckinley chadwick  started his career as a sound engineer  now after a year of pandemic he's  struggling and  unsure how he will cope if the 20 pound  a week universal credit lifeline  thrown last spring is withdrawn at the  end of march  got no money i've got like 130 quid in  the bank  it affects people people like me  people like my friends and family you  know my mom's disabled she's on  universal credit  if they cut that that's going to be a  big chunk of our food shopping that  we're not going to be able to afford  the labor leader highlighting what  losing a thousand pounds a year will  mean for many of the six million people  who were helped by the universal credit  boost we're still in the middle of a  pandemic and the government wants to get  rid of that  uplift which is vital to those families  it's the wrong thing  to do i think many tory mps in their  heart of heart  know it's the wrong thing to do he  succeeded in goading some of them to  speak out today  now's not the time to consider any  reduction in the uplift in universal  credit  this uplift was brought in to help  people to the extreme challenges of the  pandemic  and those challenges haven't passed the  uplift is so important right now and  it's why  i believe it needs to be extended for  the further 12 months  the ice to the right 278 the nose to the  left  nil the government managed to stop any  rebel tory mps voting with labour  but that doesn't mean the problem has  gone away fantastic  visiting a vaccine centre in oxford  boris johnson makes clear  no decisions have been taken what we've  said is that we'll put our arms around  the whole of the country  throughout the pandemic the countries  we've already done uh  i think 280 billion pounds worth of of  support  and we'll keep all measures obviously  under under constant review  the fact that boris johnson hasn't  automatically  sided with his chancellor is striking  rishi sunak  wants everybody to be braced for the end  of emergency support that got us through  the pandemic within months  but boris johnson can spy around with  his back benches he doesn't want to have  life is about to get more difficult for  the treasury  not least because furlough ends at the  end of april  meaning that just as a lot of people  have got vaccines  more people are going to end up on  unemployment and hence  on universal credit it's just the little  things  those like mike who lost his job in  hospitality moved back in with his  mother  and is still struggling to cope yes you  know you can live within your means  you know it's a great little phrase but  when you're already living well within  your means and  and still there's not enough money for  the month  i i don't know what to do rishi sunak is  going round whitehall telling people  that whatever he pays for now has to be  paid  back later but when will boris johnson  let him do that  sam coates sky news

